```python
class Bird:
    def fly(self):
        return "I am flying!"

class Penguin(Bird):
    def fly(self):
        raise NotImplementedError("Penguins cannot fly.")

def make_bird_fly(bird: Bird):
    print(bird.fly())

# This works
make_bird_fly(Bird())
# This violates LSP because substituting Penguin for Bird breaks the program's expected behavior (calling fly())
# make_bird_fly(Penguin()) # Will raise NotImplementedError
```
The `Penguin` class violates LSP because when substituted for `Bird`, calling its `fly()` method results in an `NotImplementedError`, which is an unexpected behavior for a client expecting any `Bird` to be able to fly. The `Penguin` strengthens the precondition (effectively, "you must not call fly()") or weakens the postcondition (doesn't return a "flying" message).
